 "params": "{path, name?, at?: [x, y], angle?: degrees} another project (.bimcraft or .ifc) shown in this one as a reference that can't be edited, as Revit's Link Revit: its walls, floors, roofs, columns, stairs and the rest of what it builds are drawn in the plans, sections and 3D views, halftone, and snapped to; nothing of it is selected, scheduled, checked or exported. It stands with its origin at `at` (default [0, 0]) turned `angle` about it; its levels stand at their own elevations, on this project's levels at the same height. `path` is read now and again by `reload`, and kept as given: a relative one is relative to this project's file. `name` defaults to the file's. Returns `id`, `name`, `elements`"
}
{
 "aliases": [],
 "id": "reload",
 "label": "Reload Links",
 "menu": null,
 "params": "{id?} read the file of the link `id`, or of every link, again: for a project that changed on disk. Returns `reloaded` (the names) and `failed` ({name, error})"
}
{
 "aliases": [],
 "id": "undo",
 "label": "Undo",
 "menu": null,
 "params": "{count?} takes back the last `count` steps (1 by default)"
}
{
 "aliases": [],
 "id": "redo",
 "label": "Redo",
 "menu": null,
 "params": "{count?} does again the last `count` steps undone (1 by default)"
}
{
 "aliases": [],
 "id": "flip",
 "label": "Flip",
 "menu": null,
 "params": "{ids: [wall,…]} turns walls round about their location lines, as Revit's flip arrows do: their exterior sides go to the other side, their doors and windows stay where they are"
}
{
 "aliases": [],
 "id": "modify",
 "label": "Modify",
 "menu": null,
 "params": "{id | ids: [id,…], …fields} change fields as `elements` shows them, of one element or several; `level` and `top_level` may be names. A filled region's or a floor's model pattern is set by `pattern_origin` [x, y], plan mm, where its lines start, and `pattern_angle`, degrees counter-clockwise (both shown by `elements`); to put a pattern line of one on a reference line, Model::align_pattern gives the `pattern_origin` that does. A floor's, a ceiling's, a roof's or a shaft's outline it sets is held to what `floor` says of it"
}
{
 "aliases": [
  "MA"
 ],
 "id": "match_properties",
 "label": "Match Properties",
 "menu": null,
 "params": "{from: id, ids: [id,…]} gives the elements in `ids` the type of `from` (and, for components, its family), as Revit's Match Properties does; only elements of `from`'s category take it, the rest come back in `skipped`. Returns {modified, skipped}"
}
{
 "aliases": [
  "AL"
 ],
 "id": "align",
 "label": "Align",
 "menu": null,
 "params": "{id, reference: [[x,y],[x,y]], line: [[x,y],[x,y]], lock?: true, to?: id} aligns an element's picked line with a reference in plan: turns by the smaller angle about the picked line's midpoint, then moves it onto the reference, keeping its size. Doors and windows slide along their host wall; they cannot turn independently. With `lock`, Revit's padlock: the line and the reference (of element `to`, else whatever has a line along it) are kept on one line after every command, as a locked dimension keeps its length; refused when that can't hold with the other locks. Returns {aligned, angle, by, line} (the picked line where it went), and the lock's id"
}
{
 "aliases": [
  "MV"
 ],
 "id": "move",
 "label": "Move",
 "menu": null,
 "params": "{ids: [id,…], by: [dx, dy], end?: start|end} moves elements in plan; walls joined to a moved wall stretch to stay joined, and doors and windows slide along their walls. With `end`, only that end of one wall moves"
}
{
 "aliases": [
  "CO"
 ],
 "id": "copy",
 "label": "Copy",
 "menu": null,
 "params": "{ids: [id,…], by: [dx, dy]} copies elements, with the doors and windows of copied walls; returns the copies' ids"
}
{
 "aliases": [
  "RO"
 ],
 "id": "rotate",
 "label": "Rotate",
 "menu": null,
 "params": "{ids: [id,…], about: [x, y], angle, copy?} turns elements about a point by `angle` degrees, counter-clockwise; walls joined to them stay joined, and doors and windows turn with their walls"
}
{
 "aliases": [
  "MM",
  "DM"
 ],
 "id": "mirror",
 "label": "Mirror",
 "menu": null,
 "params": "{ids: [id,…], axis: [[x, y], [x, y]], copy?} mirrors elements across the line through two points; mirrored doors and windows face the other way"
}
{
 "aliases": [
  "TR"
 ],
 "id": "corner",
 "label": "Trim/Extend to Corner",
 "menu": null,
 "params": "{ids: [a, b], keep?: [[x, y], [x, y]]} trims or extends two walls, or two detail lines (lines or arcs), to meet where their lines (or circles) cross; `keep` is a point on the part of each to keep"
}
{
 "aliases": [
  "SL"
 ],
 "id": "split",
 "label": "Split",
 "menu": null,
 "params": "{id, at: [x, y]} splits a wall or a detail line in two at `at`: a wall's doors and windows go with their part; a detail line of any shape splits where it passes nearest `at` (a spline by its control points exactly, one through points into two through points within 0.05 mm of it), and a closed one (a circle, a whole ellipse, a closed polyline) opens there instead, into one that starts and ends at `at`"
}
{
 "aliases": [
  "FL"
 ],
 "id": "fillet",
 "label": "Fillet Arc",
 "menu": null,
 "params": "{ids: [a, b], radius} rounds the corner where two straight detail lines, or two straight walls, meet (or would, extended) with an arc of `radius` mm, in the first's line style or of the first's type: each is cut back to the arc"
}
{
 "aliases": [
  "DE"
 ],
 "id": "delete",
 "label": "Delete",
 "menu": null,
 "params": "{ids: [id,…]} deleting a wall deletes its doors and windows, deleting those their tags; a view takes its notes and spot elevations, and what a spot elevation reads takes it; dimensions lose what's deleted"
}
{
 "aliases": [],
 "id": "delete_type",
 "label": "Delete Type",
 "menu": null,
 "params": "{category?: wall|floor|door|window|column|roof|ceiling|railing|…, family?, type?} deletes a type of a category (`category` and `type`), a family (`family`) or one of a family's types (`family` and `type`), as Revit's Delete on one in the Project Browser does; refused while an element uses it (it says how many), and for the last type of a category or of a family"
}
{
 "aliases": [],
 "id": "rename_type",
 "label": "Rename Type",
 "menu": null,
 "params": "{category?: wall|floor|door|window|column|roof|ceiling|railing|…, family?, type, name} renames a type of a category (`category` and `type`) or one of a family's types (`family` and `type`) to `name`, as Rename in Type Properties does: the elements of it, and what else names it, follow, and it keeps its GUID; refused for a name another type of it has. Text types and filled region types rename with `rename` on `text_type` and `filled_region_type`"
}
{
 "aliases": [
  "PU"
 ],
 "id": "purge",
 "label": "Purge Unused",
 "menu": "Manage > Purge Unused",
 "params": "{kinds?: [type|family|material|picture|filter|template, …], dry_run?} takes out what nothing uses, again for what that leaves unused: types and families unless `kinds` says; a category keeps a type and a family a type; with `dry_run` it lists what would go and changes nothing"
}

